#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063964 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/id #uuid "4f78dc34-7cc7-5024-9641-b4ea74e4d155" :question/math-academy-id "e-3743" :question/problem "Find the $x$-values of the stationary points of $f(x) = x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2$ and classify each point." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 16 x\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{3} - 16x &= 0 \\\\\nx^{3} - 4x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x + 2)(x - 2) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 2$.\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of $f''(x)$ at the critical points. Computing $f''(x)$, we get\n\n$$\nf''(x)= 12 x^2 - 16\n$$\n\nand evaluating $f''(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(-2) &= 12(-2)^{2} - 16 = 32 > 0 \\\\\nf^{″}(0) &= 12(0)^{2} - 16 =-16 < 0 \\\\\nf^{″}(2) &= 12(2)^{2} - 16 = 32 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=0$, so this is a relative maximum. On the other hand, the second derivative is positive at $x=\\pm 2$, so these are relative minima."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063965 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/id #uuid "088bc8a4-e82a-586b-89b5-49482f579c4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3744" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = x^3-6x^2-15x$. Given that $f'(5) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f''(5) > 0$\n2. $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$\n3. $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=5$ is a stationary point of $f(x)$. We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)= x^3-6x^2-15x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 3x^2-12x-15\n$$\n\nWe're given that $f'(5) = 0$. Indeed, we can see that\n\n$$\nf'(5) = 3(5)^2 - 12(5) - 15 = 0\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x) &= 6x - 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(5) &= 6(5) - 12 \\\\\n&= 30 - 12 \\\\\n&= 18 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e099d05-93f3-54cd-b4d7-4798641df132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061842} {:answer/id #uuid "dacbda70-1377-52f6-a5e7-055341259beb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061843} {:answer/id #uuid "e23211cb-1730-56f0-803d-e4c1bcebc97f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061844} {:answer/id #uuid "356ef0f6-7046-542b-bad0-849d97dc9474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061845} {:answer/id #uuid "41ec81cf-9788-59de-a704-623a4cc3969c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e099d05-93f3-54cd-b4d7-4798641df132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061842} :answer-field/id #uuid "6153a82b-a3cf-5d0b-abfd-d8c3da44ff45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061841}] :question/id #uuid "a4e43127-8c70-53b8-9c7f-f4ea673dfe39" :question/math-academy-id "q-2459" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFor the function $f(x) = 6x^{2} - 4x^{3}$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f^{″}(0) > 0$\n2. $x = 0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n3. $f^{″}(1) < 0$\n4. $x = 1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$" :question/requires-calculator true}]}
